msgstr ""

#: src/testing/test-registration.md:51
msgid "`cargo test`"
msgstr ""

#: src/testing/test-registration.md:53
msgid ""
"The last one is the Rust analogue and it is worth spelling out: cargo "
"auto-discovers `crates/<crate>/tests/<suite>.rs`, but a file one level "
"deeper — a suite's module directory, or a `#[cfg(test)]` submodule under "
"`src/` — only compiles if a `mod` declaration names it. Without one, rustc "
"never parses the file. It is not dead code; it is not code. No warning fires."
msgstr ""

#: src/testing/test-registration.md:59
msgid ""
"Everything else is glob-driven and cannot go dark. `--list` names those too, "
"so \"considered and safe\" is distinguishable from \"never looked at\"."
msgstr ""

#: src/testing/test-registration.md:62
msgid "What the gate does, and what it refuses to do"
msgstr ""

#: src/testing/test-registration.md:64
msgid "Per CLAUDE.md's _four ways a gate can be unable to fail_:"
msgstr ""

#: src/testing/test-registration.md:66
msgid ""
"**It cannot pass vacuously.** Every mechanism declares a floor on its "
"candidate set and fails if the glob stops matching. \"0 dark files over 0 "
"candidates\" and \"0 dark files over 157 candidates\" print the same verdict "
"and mean opposite things, so the summary always names the counts: `checked "
"157 files against 4 registries`."
msgstr ""

#: src/testing/test-registration.md:71
msgid ""
"**It is proven able to fail.** `--self-test` plants an unregistered file "
"into each of the four mechanisms — over the real registries, via an "
"in-memory overlay, so nothing touches your working tree — asserts the gate "
"names it, then removes it and asserts the gate goes green again. It also "
"asserts a collapsed candidate set fails, a stale exclusion fails, and a "
"registry entry whose file is gone fails."
msgstr ""

#: src/testing/test-registration.md:77
msgid ""
"**Exclusions are named, not counted.** A numeric threshold cannot tell a new "
"dark file from an old one: fix one, add one, and the tally is unchanged. "
"Every non-registered candidate is listed in the script with a reason. A "
"stale exclusion — one that matches no file on disk — is itself a failure, so "
"an excuse cannot outlive the file it excuses."
msgstr ""

#: src/testing/test-registration.md:82
msgid ""
"**It runs where it blocks.** It is a step in `lint`, which is already a "
"required context. That placement is deliberate: forgetting to add a new job "
"to branch protection is hazard 2, and `gc-root-dominance` sat red and "
"blocking nothing for days because of it. This gate adds no new job, so there "
"is no branch-protection step left to forget."
msgstr ""

#: src/testing/test-registration.md:88
msgid "When it fires"
msgstr ""

#: src/testing/test-registration.md:90
msgid "You will see something like:"
msgstr ""

#: src/testing/test-registration.md:102
msgid "Two ways out, and only two:"
msgstr ""

#: src/testing/test-registration.md:104
msgid ""
"**Register it.** Add the line to the named registry. This is almost always "
"the right answer — you wrote the file to run."
msgstr ""

#: src/testing/test-registration.md:106
msgid ""
"**Exclude it, with a reason.** If the file is genuinely a helper (a fixture "
"imported by a registered test, a vendored dependency, a workload driven by a "
"_different_ registry), add it to that mechanism's `exclusions` dict in "
"`scripts/check_test_registration.py` and say why in prose. Reviewers read "
"that text; \"excluded\" on its own is not an answer."
msgstr ""

#: src/testing/test-registration.md:112
msgid ""
"There is deliberately no third way. No threshold to bump, no `--allow-dark`, "
"no environment variable."
msgstr ""

#: src/testing/test-registration.md:115
msgid "Not covered"
msgstr ""

#: src/testing/test-registration.md:117
msgid ""
"`tests/*.sh`, `tests/*.py` and `tests/*.ts` have no registry to diff against "
"— 143 of the 171 files there are referenced by nothing in the tree. That is "
"a separate archaeology problem (triage each one: wire it up, or delete it), "
"not an unregistered-file problem, and inventing a registry for it "
"retroactively would make this gate red on day one for reasons that have "
"nothing to do with the four dark witnesses it was written for."
msgstr ""

