#: src/cli/cache-dir.md:1
msgid "Cache directory"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/cache-dir.md:3
msgid ""
"Where Perry writes its on-disk caches for a project. Defaults to "
"`<project-root>/node_modules/.cache/perry`, the find-cache-dir convention "
"used by babel-loader, eslint, and most of the JS toolchain."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/cache-dir.md:7
msgid ""
"Everything Perry caches lands directly under this directory: per-module "
"object files (`objects/<target>/`), the build-cache manifest (`build/`), the "
"link-cache manifest (`link/`), the behavioral SBOM (`audit.json`), "
"sandbox-exec profiles (`buildrs-<pkg>.sandbox`), and HIR miss dumps "
"(`debug/`) when `PERRY_CACHE_DEBUG_HIR=1`."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/cache-dir.md:13
msgid "Four ways to set it"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/cache-dir.md:15
msgid ""
"Precedence, highest to lowest: CLI flag, then `PERRY_CACHE_DIR`, then "
"`perry.toml`, then `package.json`. So `perry.toml` overrides `package.json`, "
"and the env var and CLI flag override `perry.toml`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/cache-dir.md:19
msgid ""
"```\n"
"# 1. Per-build CLI flag (wins over everything)\n"
"perry compile --cache-dir /var/cache/perry myapp.ts\n"
"\n"
"# 2. Per-shell environment\n"
"PERRY_CACHE_DIR=/var/cache/perry perry compile myapp.ts\n"
"\n"
"# 3. Per-project perry.toml, alongside the other [perry] settings\n"
"[perry]\n"
"cacheDir = \"/var/cache/perry\"\n"
"\n"
"# 4. Per-project package.json (most common)\n"
"{\n"
"  \"perry\": {\n"
"    \"cacheDir\": \".perry-cache\"\n"
"  }\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/cache-dir.md:38
msgid ""
"A relative path resolves against the project root, so two projects that both "
"set `cacheDir = \".cache\"` keep separate caches. An absolute path is used "
"as-is."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/cache-dir.md:44
msgid ""
"The directory is created automatically on the first build. If it can't be "
"created (read-only root, permission error), Perry silently degrades to a "
"no-op cache for that run rather than failing the build."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/cache-dir.md:47
msgid "The cache directory must be writable for caching to take effect."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/cache-dir.md:48
msgid ""
"The default `node_modules/.cache/perry` rides along with the already-ignored "
"`node_modules/`, so nothing new lands in `git status`."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/cache-dir.md:50
msgid ""
"Older Perry versions wrote to `<project-root>/.perry-cache/`. That directory "
"is now stale and can be deleted; Perry no longer reads or writes it. Run "
"`perry cache clean` to wipe the current cache."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/cache-dir.md:53
msgid ""
"An explicit `--cache-dir` is useful for CI caches (point it at a path your "
"CI restores between runs), shared build farms (a fast local SSD instead of "
"an NFS-mounted project root), and read-only project roots (relocate the "
"cache to a writable scratch dir)."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/cache-dir.md:58
msgid ""
"The object cache is machine-local — it bakes in `-mcpu=native` codegen — so "
"sharing a cache directory across machines with different CPUs (rsync, NFS, a "
"Docker bind-mount) can produce `SIGILL` at runtime. Scope a shared "
"`--cache-dir` to a single CPU family."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:1
msgid "Fast-math and FP contraction"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:3
msgid ""
"Off by default. Opt in to permit LLVM optimizations on f64 arithmetic that "
"produce observably different results from Node's V8 in exchange for faster "
"code on a narrow class of numeric workloads."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:9
msgid "Bit-exact with Node"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:9
msgid "Speed"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:11
msgid ""
"Yes (~94% of random FP programs match Node bit-for-bit; the residual ~6% "
"comes from the LLVM SLP vectorizer at `-O3`, not from fast-math)"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:11
msgid "Same as Node within noise on realistic FP code"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:12
msgid "`--fp-contract=on` or `fast`"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:12
msgid "No where FMA fusion changes rounding"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:12
msgid "Can emit FMA for multiply-add shapes without enabling reassociation"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:13
msgid ""
"No (~70%; ~30% of random FP programs diverge by 1 ULP). Implies "
"`--fp-contract=fast` unless explicitly overridden."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:13
msgid ""
"~7x faster on tight `sum += constant` loops; ~0% difference on dot products, "
"array reductions, or any data-dependent FP-heavy code (M-series ARM64 "
"numbers; x86_64 may differ)"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:15
msgid ""
"If your program does scientific computing, signal processing, or any "
"hand-tuned numeric kernel that benefits from autovectorization or FMA "
"fusion, `--fast-math` may help. For everything else (UI, business logic, "
"crypto, networking, framework code), it changes nothing observable except "
"correctness — leave it off."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:21
msgid "Three ways to enable it"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:23
msgid "CLI flag wins over env var, env var wins over package.json:"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:26
msgid "# 1. Per-build CLI flag"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:28
msgid "# 2. Per-shell environment"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:31
msgid "# 3. Per-project package.json (most common)"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:35
msgid "\"fastMath\""
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:40
msgid "Floating-point contraction"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:42
msgid "Contraction is separate from reassociation:"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:45
msgid "# Permit FMA contraction only."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:47
msgid ""
"# Permit the frontend's most aggressive contraction mode without "
"reassociation."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:50
msgid "# Keep reassociation from --fast-math but block FMA contraction."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:55
msgid ""
"The same setting is available through `PERRY_FP_CONTRACT=off|on|fast` or "
"`\"perry\": { \"fpContract\": \"on\" }` in package.json. Explicit package, "
"env, or CLI `fpContract` values override the `--fast-math` implied default."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:60
msgid "What it actually changes"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:62
msgid ""
"Two LLVM per-instruction fast-math flags can be emitted on every `fadd` / "
"`fsub` / `fmul` / `fdiv` / `frem` / `fneg`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:65
msgid ""
"**`reassoc`** — permits the optimizer to reorder associative chains. `(a + "
"b) + c` may become `a + (b + c)`. This is what the loop-vectorizer needs to "
"break a serial accumulator dependency chain into 4 parallel accumulators. "
"Worst-case observable behavior: tiny ULP-level differences in long sum "
"chains over operands of widely-different magnitudes; rewrites like `(a / b) "
"* b → (a * b) / b` (algebraically equal, IEEE-different)."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:73
msgid ""
"**`contract`** — controlled by `--fp-contract`; permits fused multiply-add. "
"`a * b + c` may become a single FMA instruction with one rounding step "
"instead of two. ARM and modern x86 both have hardware FMA. Worst-case "
"observable behavior: intermediate `a * b` no longer rounds independently, so "
"code that depends on the rounding structure (Kahan summation, compensated "
"arithmetic) sees different bits."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:81
msgid "What it deliberately does NOT enable"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:83
msgid ""
"The full clang `-ffast-math` is **off** even with `--fast-math`. In "
"particular, these flags stay clear:"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:86
msgid ""
"`nnan` / `ninf` — these tell LLVM to assume no NaN/Inf inputs, which is "
"catastrophic for Perry: NaN-boxing uses NaN bit patterns for every "
"non-number value (strings, objects, null, undefined, booleans). Enabling "
"them caused LLVM to replace `TAG_NULL` / `TAG_UNDEFINED` constants with "
"`0.0` at codegen time. Tried at v0.2.x commit `083ce16`, reverted two days "
"later in `b5a8c83f`. Will not return."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:92
msgid ""
"`nsz` (no signed zeros) — would make `(a + 0) → a` a valid rewrite even when "
"`a` is `-0`. `Object.is(-0, 0)` is observable in JS."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:94
msgid ""
"`arcp` (allow reciprocal) — would rewrite `a / b → a * (1 / b)`, which loses "
"precision when `b` is far from a power of two."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:96
msgid ""
"`afn` (approximate functions) — would let LLVM substitute lower- precision "
"math intrinsics."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:99
msgid ""
"For reference, Rust nightly's `#![feature(float_algebraic)]` enables "
"`reassoc + contract + nsz + arcp`. Perry's `--fast-math` is strictly more "
"conservative than that."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:103
msgid "Performance numbers"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:105
msgid ""
"Benchmarks on Apple Silicon (M-series, ARM64), `min` of 3 runs each, LLVM "
"19, perry 0.5.569. Run `scripts/perf_bench.sh` to reproduce."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:108
msgid "Benchmark"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:108
msgid "Ratio"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:108
msgid "Node"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:110
msgid "`sum_loop` (100M `sum += 1`)"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:110
msgid "96 ms"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:110 src/cli/fast-math.md:111
msgid "13 ms"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:110
msgid "**7.4× faster**"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:110
msgid "53 ms"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:111
msgid "`dot_product` (10M `sum += a[i]*b[i]`)"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:111 src/cli/fast-math.md:112
msgid "1.00×"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:111
msgid "12 ms"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:112
msgid "`array_sum` (10M `sum += xs[i]`)"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:112
msgid "10 ms"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:112
msgid "11 ms"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:114
msgid ""
"Read these together: `--fast-math` produces a large speedup ONLY on loops "
"where the accumulator step is constant or trivially-redundant enough that "
"LLVM can split it into parallel partial sums. Real FP workloads rarely look "
"like `sum += 1` and so rarely benefit. The default mode beats Node on "
"`array_sum` and matches it on `dot_product` without giving up bit-exact "
"parity."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:121
msgid "Correctness numbers"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:123
msgid ""
"`scripts/fp_fuzz.mjs` — randomly generates TS programs exercising the six "
"patterns most likely to trip per-instruction FMFs (left-fold, tree-fold, "
"right-fold reductions; FMA-shaped chains; algebraic identities like "
"`(a/b)*b`; cancellation predicates). Each program is compiled with both Node "
"and Perry, and stdout is diffed byte-for-byte."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:129
msgid "Pass rate (100 random programs, seed=200)"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:131
msgid "94/100"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:132
msgid "~70/100"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:134
msgid ""
"The 6/100 default-mode failures are residual divergences from sources not "
"gated by per-instruction FMFs — most originate in the LLVM SLP vectorizer at "
"`-O3`, which can apply pairwise reduction even without the `reassoc` "
"permission. Tracked separately; out of scope for this flag."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:140
msgid "Object-cache interaction"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:142
msgid ""
"Perry's per-module `.o` cache (in `node_modules/.cache/perry/objects/`, or "
"wherever `--cache-dir` points) keys on the `fast_math` and `fp_contract` "
"settings alongside source hash and other compile options. Toggling either "
"invalidates affected cache entries — `perry --fast-math` or `perry "
"--fp-contract=on` right after `perry` does a clean recompile of every module "
"that contains f64 arithmetic. No `--no-cache` necessary."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:150
msgid ""
"(This is a deliberate fix. During the original investigation, an early "
"version of the flag forgot to enter the cache key, and the result was that "
"toggling the flag appeared to do nothing because all `.o` files came from "
"the cache. If you ever see fast-math defaults that _seem_ not to take "
"effect, suspect the cache key first.)"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:156
msgid "Migration notes"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:158
msgid ""
"**For library authors:** if your TS library publishes benchmark numbers, "
"document which mode you measured under. The 7× sum-loop case is the only "
"place the gap is large; if your benchmark doesn't look like that, the "
"numbers are mode-independent and you can publish one set."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:163
msgid ""
"**For app authors:** there is no migration. Default behavior is the pre-flag "
"behavior with `--fast-math` removed; bit-exact results are _more_ compatible "
"with Node, not less."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/fast-math.md:166
msgid ""
"**For determinism-critical code** (lockstep simulations, financial "
"reconciliation, hash function correctness): leave the default. Even with "
"`--fast-math` off there's a residual ~6% divergence rate on random FP code, "
"which is too high for true determinism work — but it's an order of magnitude "
"better than the ~30% with the flag on."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/dynamic-dispatch.md:1
msgid "Dynamic Stdlib Dispatch (`--lockdown`)"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/dynamic-dispatch.md:3
msgid ""
"Perry can refuse compile-time _dynamic dispatch_ on Node-core stdlib "
"namespaces. A call site like"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/dynamic-dispatch.md:6
msgid ""
"```typescript,no-test\n"
"const m = \"exit\";\n"
"(process as any)[m](0);\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/dynamic-dispatch.md:11
msgid ""
"is the standard string-based obfuscation pattern used by malicious npm "
"packages: `process[\"bind\" + \"ing\"](\"dns\")`, "
"`globalThis[atob(\"ZXZhbA==\")]()`, `fs[methodName]()` where `methodName` is "
"computed at runtime."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/dynamic-dispatch.md:15
msgid ""
"**Default: allowed.** Since "
"[\\#5263](https://github.com/PerryTS/perry/issues/5263) the refusal is _off_ "
"by default. Dynamic `fs[name]` over a namespace Perry has already statically "
"linked can only _select among the methods that were linked_ — it is dynamic "
"selection of a known set, not a way to reach arbitrary code — so it is safe "
"to allow, and legitimate packages depend on it (graceful-fs stores its retry "
"queue on `fs[Symbol.for('graceful-fs.queue')]`; fs-extra wraps the known "
"`fs[method]` functions). Dynamic reads resolve the linked member by name; "
"writes the program performs persist and read back — **string** keys via a "
"module-keyed override side-table, **symbol** keys on the cached namespace "
"object (so graceful-fs's `fs[Symbol.for('graceful-fs.queue')] = queue` "
"round-trips)."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/dynamic-dispatch.md:26
msgid ""
"**The refusal is re-armed by [`--lockdown`](./lockdown.md)** — the "
"supply-chain gate — and by an explicit opt-out (below). Under those, the "
"site below fails to compile with `error[U006]`. The pass is purely "
"compile-time (**zero runtime cost**). Issue "
"[\\#503](https://github.com/PerryTS/perry/issues/503) tracks the original "
"design."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/dynamic-dispatch.md:32
msgid "What's checked (when the refusal is armed)"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/dynamic-dispatch.md:34
msgid ""
"The refusal is armed under `--lockdown` (or `perry.lockdown: true` / "
"`PERRY_LOCKDOWN=1`), or by an explicit `perry.allowDynamicStdlibDispatch: "
"false` / `PERRY_ALLOW_DYNAMIC_STDLIB=0`. When armed, dynamic dispatch is "
"refused when **all** of the following hold:"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/dynamic-dispatch.md:39
msgid ""
"The receiver resolves to a known Node-core stdlib namespace: `process`, "
"`fs`, `crypto`, `child_process`, `net`, `os`, `path`, `http`, `https`, "
"`http2`, `stream`, `url`, `util`, `events`, `dns`, `tls`, `querystring`, "
"`zlib`, `async_hooks`, `readline`, `string_decoder`, `tty`, `worker_threads`."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/dynamic-dispatch.md:44
msgid ""
"The index expression is _not_ a string literal — `fs[\"readFileSync\"]` is "
"treated identically to `fs.readFileSync` and always passes."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/dynamic-dispatch.md:46
msgid "The user has not opted out (see below)."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/dynamic-dispatch.md:48
msgid "User-code reflection on user-defined objects is unaffected:"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/dynamic-dispatch.md:50
msgid ""
"```typescript,no-test\n"
"const me = { greet: (n: string) => \"hi \" + n };\n"
"const k = \"greet\";\n"
"me[k](\"world\"); // ✓ user object, not a stdlib namespace\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/dynamic-dispatch.md:56
msgid "Opt-outs (when armed)"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/dynamic-dispatch.md:58
msgid ""
"When the refusal is armed and a site is refused, the error message lists the "
"available opt-outs in priority order:"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/dynamic-dispatch.md:61
msgid "1. Replace with a static call"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/dynamic-dispatch.md:63
msgid ""
"The preferred fix. The check exists precisely because static calls are "
"auditable."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/dynamic-dispatch.md:66
msgid ""
"```typescript,no-test\n"
"process.exit(0);                // ✓\n"
"fs.readFileSync(\"/tmp/x\");       // ✓\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/dynamic-dispatch.md:71
msgid "2. `// @perry-allow-dynamic` annotation (host code only)"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/dynamic-dispatch.md:73
msgid ""
"For legitimate one-off dispatch in your own code, drop a line comment on or "
"immediately above the offending site:"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/dynamic-dispatch.md:76
msgid ""
"```typescript,no-test\n"
"const k = pickHandler();\n"
"// @perry-allow-dynamic\n"
"(process as any)[k](0);\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/dynamic-dispatch.md:82
msgid ""
"Contiguous comment lines above the call also count, so the annotation can "
"sit alongside an `// @ts-ignore` or similar."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/dynamic-dispatch.md:85
msgid ""
"The annotation is honored **only in host source files** (anything not under "
"`node_modules/`). A dependency cannot grant itself the opt-out by writing "
"`// @perry-allow-dynamic` next to its own call — that would defeat the "
"supply-chain defense the check exists for. Dependencies opt in via the "
"host's per-package allow list (below) or the global flag. This host-only "
"rule was implemented by closed issue "
"[\\#996](https://github.com/PerryTS/perry/issues/996)."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/dynamic-dispatch.md:93
msgid "3. Per-package allow list in `package.json`"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/dynamic-dispatch.md:95
msgid ""
"To opt one or more npm dependencies out, list them under "
"`perry.allowDynamicStdlibDispatch` in the **host** application's "
"`package.json`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/dynamic-dispatch.md:102 src/cli/dynamic-dispatch.md:117
msgid "\"allowDynamicStdlibDispatch\""
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/dynamic-dispatch.md:102
msgid "\"legacy-dep\""
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/dynamic-dispatch.md:102
msgid "\"@scope/other-dep\""
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/dynamic-dispatch.md:107
msgid ""
"Modules whose source path lives under `node_modules/<pkg>/…` are matched "
"against this list. Host code is _not_ covered — opting host code out "
"requires the global flag below or the site annotation."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/dynamic-dispatch.md:112
msgid "4. Global opt-out"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/dynamic-dispatch.md:114
msgid "To disable the check across the entire build, set the boolean form:"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/dynamic-dispatch.md:120
msgid "…or set the env var for a one-off build:"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/dynamic-dispatch.md:126
msgid ""
"CI can enforce the check by setting `PERRY_ALLOW_DYNAMIC_STDLIB=0`, which "
"beats any package.json opt-out."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/dynamic-dispatch.md:129
msgid "Why allowed by default (and gated under lockdown)"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/dynamic-dispatch.md:131
msgid ""
"Dynamic member access over a _linked_ stdlib namespace can only reach the "
"methods Perry already linked statically — it is dynamic _selection_ among a "
"known set, not a way to construct or reach arbitrary code. The "
"dispatch-by-string obfuscation it could otherwise hide is only meaningful "
"when paired with the arbitrary-code surfaces that `--lockdown` already "
"forbids (`eval`/`Function`, `child_process`, native archives). So the "
"refusal belongs with the rest of the lockdown gate, not always-on: default "
"builds allow it (so graceful-fs, fs-extra, and similar legitimate patterns "
"compile), while security-sensitive builds opt into `--lockdown` and get the "
"refusal back. An explicit `perry.allowDynamicStdlibDispatch: false` / "
"`PERRY_ALLOW_DYNAMIC_STDLIB=0` re-arms just this check without the rest of "
"lockdown."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/dynamic-dispatch.md:143
msgid ""
"See [`#503`](https://github.com/PerryTS/perry/issues/503) for design "
"discussion and the broader supply-chain hardening series "
"(\\[`#495`–`#506`\\] "
"(https://github.com/PerryTS/perry/issues?q=is%3Aissue+label%3Aenhancement+security))."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/allow-js-runtime.md:1
msgid "JS Runtime Opt-In (`perry.allowJsRuntime`)"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/allow-js-runtime.md:3
msgid ""
"Perry refuses to link `perry-jsruntime` — the QuickJS-based runtime that "
"executes interpreted `.js` files from `node_modules/` — unless the host "
"application has explicitly opted in. This protects Perry's primary "
"structural advantage over Node: a Perry binary normally contains _no_ JS "
"evaluator at all."
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/allow-js-runtime.md:9
msgid "The check fires at compile time. **Zero runtime cost.**"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/allow-js-runtime.md:11 src/cli/allow-perry-features.md:14
msgid "How a build hits this"
msgstr ""

#: src/cli/allow-js-runtime.md:13
msgid ""
"The Perry compiler routes any `.js`/`.cjs`/`.mjs` file from `node_modules/` "
"through `perry-jsruntime`'s QuickJS sandbox instead of the native LLVM "
"backend. Most npm packages are pure-JS, so transitive deps can pull the "
"runtime in without the host author noticing — a silent regression of Perry's "
"main hardening pitch."
